#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:17
#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:18
#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:101
#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:104
msgid "\"{:?}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:22
msgid "Confirm understanding of the syntax"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:24
msgid ""
"`Box<i32>` type is a reference to an integer on the heap that is owned by "
"the box."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:26
msgid ""
"`*mut i32` type is a so-called raw pointer to an integer that the compiler "
"does not know the ownership of. Programmers need to ensure the rules are "
"enforced without assistance from the compiler."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:29
msgid ""
"Note: raw pointers do not provide ownership info to Rust. A pointer can be "
"semantically owning the data, or semantically borrowing, but that "
"information only exists in the programmer's mind."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:33
msgid "`&mut *boxed as *mut _` expression:"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:34
msgid "`*boxed` is ..."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:35
msgid "`&mut *boxed` is ..."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:36
msgid "finally, `as *mut i32` casts the reference to a pointer."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:38
msgid ""
"References, such as `&mut i32`, \"borrow\" their referent. This is Rust's "
"ownership system."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:41
msgid "Confirm understanding of ownership"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:43
msgid "Step through code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:44
msgid ""
"(Line 3) Creates raw pointer to the `123` by dereferencing the box, creating "
"a new reference and casting the new reference as a pointer."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:46
msgid "(Line 4) Creates raw pointer with a NULL value"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:47
msgid "(Line 7) Converts the raw pointer to an Option with `.as_mut()`"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:49
msgid "Highlight that pointers are nullable in Rust (unlike references)."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:51
msgid "Compile to reveal the error messages."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:53
msgid "Discuss"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:54
msgid "(Line 6) `println!(\"{:?}\", *a);`"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:55
msgid "Prefix star dereferences a raw pointer."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:56
msgid ""
"It is an explicit operation. Whereas regular references have implicit "
"dereferencing most of the time thanks to the Deref trait. This is referred "
"to as \"auto-deref\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:59
msgid "Dereferencing a raw pointer is an unsafe operation."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:60
msgid "Requires an unsafe block."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:61
msgid "(Line 7) `println!(\"{:?}\", b.as_mut());`"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:62
msgid "`as_mut()` is an unsafe function."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:63
msgid "Calling an unsafe function requires an unsafe block."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:65
msgid ""
"Demonstrate: Fix the code (add unsafe blocks) and compile again to show the "
"working program."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:68
msgid ""
"Demonstrate: Replace `as *mut i32` with `as *mut _`, show that it compiles."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:70
msgid ""
"We can partially omit the target type in the cast. The Rust compiler knows "
"that the source of the cast is a `&mut i32`. This reference type can only be "
"converted to one pointer type, `*mut i32`."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:74
msgid "Add safety comments:"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:75
msgid ""
"We said that the unsafe code marks the responsibility shift from the "
"compiler to the programmer."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:77
msgid ""
"How do we convey that we thought about our unusual responsibilities while "
"writing unsafe code? Safety comments."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:79
msgid "Safety comments explain why unsafe code is correct."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:80
msgid "Without a safety comment, unsafe code is not safe."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:82
msgid "Discuss: Whether to use one large unsafe block or two smaller ones:"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:83
msgid "Possibility of using a single unsafe block rather than multiple."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:84
msgid "Using more allows safety comments as specific as possible."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:88
msgid "_Suggested Solution_"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:99
msgid ""
"// SAFETY: `a` is a non-null pointer to i32, it is initialized and still\n"
"    // allocated.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-deep-dive/safety-preconditions/references.md:103
msgid ""
"// SAFETY: `b` is a null pointer, which `as_mut()` converts to `None`.\n"
msgstr ""
